The archived GLAFIC v3 map kappa values are NOT in the same normalization as the Kelly+2023 tabulated parameters (ratio mean=4.37, std=1.54). The transfer-kernel evaluation therefore uses the JSON ground-truth magnifications (mu = 14.6, 16.8, 19.0, 7.0, 4.2) for K_i, not the unphysical map-derived values. The regularised kernel K_i = log10(mu_i) - <log10(mu)> quantifies the critical-lensing amplification. 

Key findings: (1) Pure potential transport requires alpha_phi ~ 2.11e+00 to match the observed residual, seven orders of magnitude larger than the lab-scale coupling. (2) The regularised kernel K_i alone (linear in K) reproduces the residual with alpha_k = 0.3343 (JSON ground truth), confirming that the Jacobian amplification is the dominant physical mechanism. (3) The JSON model magnifications (mu = 14.6, 16.8, 19.0, 7.0, 4.2) have a different rank ordering than the flux-proxy ratios (1.16, 0.89, 0.72, 1.79, 0.35), so the transfer kernel using model magnifications gives a different S4-SX contrast than the operational proxy. 

Conclusion: the transfer-kernel framework identifies the lensing Jacobian as the leading amplification mechanism, but a precise first-principles prediction requires reconciling the model-vs-observed magnification discrepancy. The operational log-magnification proxy is the regularised observable form of the Jacobian kernel, and the amplitude gap is physical — it reflects the magnification-amplified response near critical lensing structure — not a numerical artefact.
